WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Feedback loop questions turn up in HSC Biology

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.400
again and again, in different disguises.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.040
Most of them come down to three moves.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.120
Build the loop in order, read it off

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.120
a graph, and tell negative feedback from positive.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.080
Here are three HSC-style questions.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.480
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.440
through it together.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:23.546
Question one.

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:26.986
When a person stands up quickly, blood pools

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:28.906
in the legs, and blood pressure in the

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:30.746
upper body falls.

00:00:30.827 --> 00:00:33.786
Baroreceptors, stretch receptors in the walls of the

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:37.386
aorta and carotid arteries, detect the fall.

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.906
The cardiovascular centre in the medulla oblongata sends

00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:43.786
nerve impulses to the heart, which beats faster

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:47.506
and more strongly, and to arterioles, which narrow.

00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:51.386
Construct a negative feedback loop for this response.

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:54.106
Then predict what would happen on standing if

00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:57.146
the nerves from the baroreceptors were damaged.

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:58.506
Pause here to answer.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:11.413
Build it in order, and take each part

00:01:11.494 --> 00:01:12.213
from the stem.

00:01:12.694 --> 00:01:15.413
The stimulus is the change in the variable:

00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:16.773
blood pressure falls.

00:01:17.174 --> 00:01:19.413
The receptors are the baroreceptors.

00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:22.653
The control centre is the cardiovascular centre in

00:01:22.694 --> 00:01:23.453
the medulla.

00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:26.373
The effectors are the heart and the arterioles.

00:01:27.014 --> 00:01:29.573
Now the response, which is where answers often

00:01:29.654 --> 00:01:30.573
fall short.

00:01:30.854 --> 00:01:33.973
A faster, stronger heartbeat pumps more blood each

00:01:34.054 --> 00:01:37.573
minute, and narrower arterioles hold the pressure up,

00:01:37.654 --> 00:01:40.613
so blood pressure rises back towards normal.

00:01:40.774 --> 00:01:42.773
Then close the loop with an arrow back

00:01:42.814 --> 00:01:46.053
to the stimulus, marked minus, because the response

00:01:46.134 --> 00:01:47.413
opposes the fall.

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:49.953
Now the prediction.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:53.193
If the baroreceptor nerves are damaged, the medulla

00:01:53.314 --> 00:01:55.593
gets no signal that pressure has fallen.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:58.873
So the heart and arterioles don't respond, and

00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:01.353
blood pressure stays low after standing.

00:02:01.914 --> 00:02:04.153
Less blood reaches the brain, so the person

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.833
may feel dizzy or faint.

00:02:06.394 --> 00:02:09.273
Break any link, and the loop can't correct.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.460
The classic mistake is ending at the effector,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.900
with "response: the heart beats faster".

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.580
That's what the effector does.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.060
The response is the change in the regulated

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.860
variable: blood pressure rising back.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:25.946
Question two.

00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:28.746
A healthy adult ate a meal at time

00:02:28.907 --> 00:02:31.386
zero, after an overnight fast.

00:02:31.707 --> 00:02:33.946
The graph shows their blood glucose and blood

00:02:34.027 --> 00:02:36.666
insulin over the next three hours.

00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:39.466
Identify when each one peaked, and account for

00:02:39.547 --> 00:02:40.826
the difference.

00:02:40.907 --> 00:02:43.866
Explain the change in blood glucose from forty-five

00:02:43.947 --> 00:02:46.346
to one hundred and twenty minutes, with reference

00:02:46.427 --> 00:02:48.106
to negative feedback.

00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:50.826
Then explain the rise in glucose after one

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:52.666
hundred and fifty minutes.

00:02:52.827 --> 00:02:54.106
Pause here to answer.

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.613
Start with the peaks, and read them off

00:03:06.654 --> 00:03:07.613
the axes.

00:03:07.894 --> 00:03:10.613
Glucose peaks at thirty minutes, at seven point

00:03:10.694 --> 00:03:12.613
six millimoles per litre.

00:03:12.694 --> 00:03:15.813
Insulin peaks later, at forty-five minutes.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:18.213
That order is the loop at work.

00:03:18.254 --> 00:03:20.773
The rise in glucose is the stimulus.

00:03:20.854 --> 00:03:23.093
Beta cells in the pancreas detect it and

00:03:23.294 --> 00:03:27.173
release insulin, so insulin climbs after glucose does.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.560
Now forty-five to one hundred and twenty minutes.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.960
Insulin is high, so liver, muscle and fat

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.760
cells take up more glucose from the blood,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.680
and the liver and muscles store it as

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:40.400
glycogen.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.840
Blood glucose falls back to about five.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.640
As it falls, the beta cells detect less

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.440
of a rise and release less insulin, so

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.720
insulin falls too.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.880
The response opposed the rise: negative feedback.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.160
After one hundred and fifty minutes, glucose has

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.240
dipped just below its starting value.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.360
The graph doesn't show glucagon, so this part

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.480
comes from what you know.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.240
Alpha cells detect the fall and release glucagon,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.720
and the liver breaks down glycogen and releases

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.760
glucose, so blood glucose rises back.

00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:17.846
The classic mistake is writing that insulin breaks

00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:19.166
down glucose.

00:04:19.447 --> 00:04:21.206
Insulin is the signal.

00:04:21.447 --> 00:04:23.806
The liver, muscle and fat cells are the

00:04:23.847 --> 00:04:26.246
effectors that take glucose in.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:28.806
Link each hormone to the cells it acts

00:04:28.887 --> 00:04:29.286
on.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.460
Question three.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.700
Three processes are described.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.700
In P, soil dries, root and leaf cells

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.620
make more ABA, the stomata close, and water

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.900
loss falls.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.420
In Q, during a long run, blood glucose

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.260
falls, the pancreas releases glucagon, the liver releases

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.140
glucose, and blood glucose rises back.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.380
In R, a ripening banana gives off a

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.460
gas called ethylene, which makes it and nearby

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.820
bananas ripen faster and give off more ethylene.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.260
Identify the process that shows positive feedback.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.540
Then explain why a student who calls Q

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.220
positive feedback, because glucose increases, is wrong.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.140
Pause here to answer.

00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:28.766
Test each process with one question: does the

00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:31.646
response oppose the change, or strengthen it?

00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:35.566
In P, water content was falling, and closing

00:05:35.647 --> 00:05:37.486
the stomata slows the loss.

00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:39.606
That opposes the change.

00:05:39.887 --> 00:05:44.206
In R, ethylene speeds ripening, ripening releases more

00:05:44.487 --> 00:05:47.326
ethylene, and that speeds ripening further.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:50.446
The response strengthens the change, so R is

00:05:50.527 --> 00:05:51.886
positive feedback.

00:05:51.967 --> 00:05:54.206
It keeps building until the fruit is ripe.

00:05:55.434 --> 00:05:56.393
Now Q.

00:05:56.874 --> 00:05:58.873
The student has looked only at which way

00:05:58.914 --> 00:06:02.153
the variable moved, up, instead of comparing the

00:06:02.474 --> 00:06:04.073
response with the stimulus.

00:06:04.394 --> 00:06:07.113
The stimulus was a fall in blood glucose.

00:06:07.354 --> 00:06:11.033
The response, glucagon making the liver release glucose,

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:12.073
raises it back.

00:06:12.714 --> 00:06:15.433
A response that undoes the change is negative

00:06:15.514 --> 00:06:18.473
feedback, whichever direction the variable moves.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.660
So the classic mistake is reading positive as

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.660
"goes up" and negative as "goes down".

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.700
Negative means the response opposes the change.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.860
Positive means it adds to it.

00:06:33.067 --> 00:06:34.986
So here's how to start each type.

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.786
To build a loop, take the five parts

00:06:37.827 --> 00:06:39.786
in order from the stem, and make the

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:41.866
response a change in the variable.

00:06:42.427 --> 00:06:45.546
To read a graph, find the stimulus first,

00:06:45.627 --> 00:06:47.226
then the response that follows it.

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:50.666
To spot positive feedback, compare the response with

00:06:50.747 --> 00:06:52.146
the original change.

00:06:52.347 --> 00:06:55.706
Opposes is negative, adds to it is positive.

00:06:56.427 --> 00:06:59.626
Next, you'll investigate how plants lose water, in

00:06:59.707 --> 00:07:00.506
a practical.

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:03.173
Here are the key notes.

00:07:03.414 --> 00:07:05.333
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:05.414 --> 00:07:06.213
to copy them.
